    <statement><p>By using the method of least squares, find the best line through the 
points:</p>
<p><m>(2, 0)</m>,   
<m>(-1, 1)</m>,   
<m>(-2, 1)</m>.</p>
<p><alert> Step 1.</alert>  The general equation of a line is 
 <m>c_0 + c_1x = y</m>.  Plugging the data points into this formula 
gives a matrix equation <m>\mathrm{A}\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{y}</m>.</p>
<p><fillin cols="2" rows="3"/> <m>\left[\begin{matrix}c_0\\c_1\end{matrix}\right]</m>
   <m>=</m>  <fillin rows="3"/></p>
<p><alert> Step 2.</alert> The matrix equation 
<m>\mathrm{A}\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{y}</m> has 
no solution, so instead we use the <alert>normal equation</alert>
<m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathrm{A}\, \hat{\mathbf{c}} = \mathrm{A^T} \mathbf{y}</m></p>
<p><m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathrm{A} =</m> <fillin cols="2" rows="2"/></p>
<p><m>\mathrm{A^T} \mathbf{y} =</m> <fillin rows="2"/></p>
<p><alert> Step 3.</alert> Solving the normal equation gives the answer</p>
<p><m>\hat{\mathbf{c}} =</m> <fillin rows="2"/></p>
<p>which corresponds to the formula</p>
<p><m>y =</m> <fillin characters="50" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
<p><alert> Analysis.</alert>  Compute the predicted <m>y</m> values: 
<m>\hat{\mathbf{y}} = \mathrm{A}\hat{\mathbf{c}}</m>.</p>
<p><m>\hat{\mathbf{y}} =</m> 
<fillin rows="3"/></p>
<p>Compute the error vector: 
<m>\mathbf{e} = \mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}}</m>.</p>
<p><m>\mathbf{e} =</m> <fillin rows="3"/></p>
<p>Compute the total error:
<m>\mathrm{SSE} = e_1^2+e_2^2+e_3^2</m>.</p>
<p><m>\mathrm{SSE} =</m>
<fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0009"/></p>
